{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2437","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2437","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2437\",\"parent_revision\":\"322f512134dccfdbf16155a51e6f20af31f3b117914f2c3dcb99744689c56019\",\"root_revision\":\"322f512134dccfdbf16155a51e6f20af31f3b117914f2c3dcb99744689c56019\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-05-25\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nPhase 3 of add-new-feature routes through dh:task-worker with a specialist profile loaded via profile_load instead of dispatching directly to a dedicated architect agent. This causes the orchestrator to read Phase 1 and Phase 2 agent outputs inline into its own context window, process them, and inject conclusions into the Phase 3 prompt as guiding facts \u2014 analysis that belongs to the architect, not the orchestrator.\\n\\nIn a live session this caused: wrong path arithmetic injected as stated facts, a fabricated open question about adding rich to PEP 723 dependencies (a project policy violation per .claude/rules/language-conventions.md), and errors the architect had to self-correct. The task-worker intermediary also loses the specialist agent's own methodology for verifying claims against primary sources before writing a spec.\\n\\nFix: dispatch Phase 3 directly to the specialist architect agent with only artifact IDs (Phase 1 feature-context, Phase 2 codebase-analysis), the issue selector, and domain skills. The architect reads those artifacts itself, checks claims against project rules, and produces the spec. The orchestrator never reads artifact content between phases \u2014 it only tracks that artifacts exist via artifact_list and passes their identifiers forward.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM: Phase 1 and Phase 2 agents return their full findings as inline text to the orchestrator\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE: SKILL.md line 173 explicitly documents Phase 2: \\\"The agent returns the content in its response; the orchestrator then registers it as an artifact.\\\" Phase 1 template (lines 142-147) similarly shows artifact registration metadata but not a content-passing mechanism, implying orchestrator reads inline.\\nSOURCE: plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md:173, lines 142-147 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\nCLAIM: Phase 3 already has the correct contract \u2014 orchestrator receives only STATUS: DONE (fixed in commit 9ef49696, issue #2429)\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE: Phase 3 delegation template (lines 365-381) explicitly requires agent to call artifact_register(content=...) and return only \\\"STATUS: DONE + path\\\". Commit 9ef49696 confirmed with exact message: \\\"refactor(add-new-feature): Phase 3 architect agent writes spec to disk and self-registers artifact (#2429)\\\". Line 533 confirms: \\\"the architect agent to call `artifact_register(content=...)` directly... the orchestrator receives only `STATUS: DONE`.\\\"\\nSOURCE: plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md:365-381, line 533; commit 9ef49696 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\nCLAIM: In a live session on #613 this caused: wrong path arithmetic injected as stated fact, and a fabricated open question about adding rich to PEP 723 dependencies\\nVERDICT: INCONCLUSIVE\\nEVIDENCE: The skill file does not document this incident. Verification requires checking GitHub issue #613 or session logs from that work, which are not accessible from the SKILL.md source.\\nSOURCE: Unable to verify without access to GitHub issue #613 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\nCLAIM: The add-new-feature skill already states this contract for Phase 3: 'the orchestrator receives only STATUS: DONE'\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE: Line 533 in the \\\"Discovered During Implementation\\\" section states exactly: \\\"the orchestrator receives only `STATUS: DONE`.\\\" Lines 379-380 show the Phase 3 return pattern with STATUS: DONE.\\nSOURCE: plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md:533, lines 379-380 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\nCLAIM: Phase 1 delegation prompt template has an artifact_register call block\\nVERDICT: REFUTED\\nEVIDENCE: Lines 142-147 show Phase 1 template with only metadata specification (artifact_type, artifact_id, item_id, agent) \u2014 no full artifact_register() call block with content parameter. Phase 3 (lines 367-375) shows the correct pattern with explicit mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_register(...) call. Phase 1 lacks this.\\nSOURCE: plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md:142-147 vs lines 367-375 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\nCLAIM: Phase 2 line 173 says 'The agent returns the content in its response; the orchestrator then registers it as an artifact'\\nVERDICT: REFUTED (STALE PROSE)\\nEVIDENCE: Line 173 literally states this exact text. However, this contradicts the actual Phase 2 delegation template (lines 188-195) which shows artifact_register responsibility delegated to the agent, and line 206 which explicitly states: \\\"The orchestrator MUST NOT call `artifact_register` as a workaround \u2014 the MCP-native rule is that agents own their artifact storage.\\\" Line 173 is documented as stale prose describing the pre-fix pattern.\\nSOURCE: plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md:173 (contradicted by lines 188-195, 206) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK: Line 533 confirms Phase 3 fixed exactly this issue \u2014 moving from \\\"orchestrator receives content inline\\\" to \\\"orchestrator receives only STATUS: DONE\\\". Phase 1 and Phase 2 still use the old pattern (line 173 documents this), creating the inconsistency the backlog item describes.\\nSOURCE: plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md:533 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Phase 3 has been correctly refactored to use artifact_register(content=...) with agent returning only STATUS: DONE. However, Phase 1 and Phase 2 still require agents to return full findings as inline text to the orchestrator, creating the data bloat and path injection problem described in the item. The skill documents this inconsistency is intentional (line 533 notes Phase 3 as \\\"Discovered During Implementation\\\"), but does not state whether Phase 1 and Phase 2 will be refactored to match.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: refactor: add-new-feature orchestrator must not read or inject artifact content between phases\\nDate: 2026-05-24\\nGoal: Update Phase 1 and Phase 2 delegation prompt templates in add-new-feature/SKILL.md to instruct agents to self-register artifacts and return only STATUS: DONE, eliminating inline content return to the orchestrator.\\nConditions:\\n1. Phase 2 delegation template instructs orchestrator-side registration (line 173 stale prose) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker verified \u2014 SKILL.md:173 confirmed present; text reads \\\"The agent returns the content in its response; the orchestrator then registers it as an artifact\\\" (VERIFIED)\\n2. Correct self-registration pattern established by Phase 3 fix | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker verified \u2014 commit 9ef49696 confirmed; Phase 3 lines 365-381 contain explicit artifact_register(content=...) + \\\"STATUS: DONE\\\\npath: plan/architect-{slug}.md\\\" (VERIFIED)\\n3. Phase 1 delegation template terminal output contract (what Phase 1 agents must return) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: technical-researcher + fact-checker \u2014 SKILL.md lines 109-158 read; Phase 1 template contains metadata-only block (artifact_type, artifact_id, item_id, agent) with no full artifact_register(content=...) call and no \\\"return only STATUS: DONE\\\" instruction; terminal output contract is undefined (VERIFIED)\\n4. Orchestrator post-dispatch instructions for Phase 1 and Phase 2 | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 orchestrator verification sections at lines 152-157 and 198-207 already say \\\"orchestrator MUST NOT call artifact_register\\\"; agent files feature-researcher.md and codebase-analyzer.md already specify STATUS: DONE in their own output contracts (VERIFIED)\\n5. Whether any other files instruct Phase 1/2 inline return | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 grep across plugins/development-harness/ found no other files with \\\"returns content in its response\\\" or \\\"orchestrator then registers\\\" patterns; fix is fully contained to SKILL.md:173 and Phase 1 template terminal instruction gap (VERIFIED)\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 3: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (fact-checker read SKILL.md lines 109-158 \u2014 Phase 1 has metadata-only, no STATUS: DONE)\\n- Condition 4: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (impact-analyst confirmed agent files and orchestrator sections)\\n- Condition 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (impact-analyst grep \u2014 no other files found)\\nAVAILABLE count: 5\\nDERIVABLE count: 0\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md::Phase 1 delegation template` \u2014 The Phase 1 delegation prompt template (lines 113\u2013147) ends with the `artifact_register` call block but has no explicit \\\"return only `STATUS: DONE`\\\" instruction. The orchestrator receives whatever the agent returns, potentially including inline content. The fix adds the `STATUS: DONE` return instruction at the end of the Phase 1 template, mirroring Phase 3 lines 365\u2013381. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Missing terminal output contract causes orchestrator context pollution \u2014 the root-cause bug documented in the item description.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md::Phase 2 line 173` \u2014 Line 173 states \\\"The agent returns the content in its response; the orchestrator then registers it as an artifact.\\\" This contradicts the Phase 2 delegation template (lines 186\u2013195) which instructs the agent to call `artifact_register` itself, and contradicts the Phase 2 re-dispatch guidance (lines 204\u2013207) which explicitly states the orchestrator MUST NOT call `artifact_register` as a workaround. The fix removes or replaces line 173 with prose consistent with agent-owned registration. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Contradictory prose \u2014 an AI orchestrator reading the skill will resolve the contradiction arbitrarily; the stale line 173 favors the wrong resolution.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md::Discovered During Implementation appendix` \u2014 Lines 530\u2013538 document the STATUS: DONE-only contract and artifact_register(content=...) pattern, but only reference the Phase 3 (architect) problem. After the fix, this appendix becomes partially stale \u2014 it says the pre-#1527 fix caused the problem but does not mention Phase 1 and Phase 2. Does not need a blocker update but is a documentation improvement opportunity (editorial only, not a correctness risk). | Risk: LOW | Why: Appendix is historical context, not an operational contract. An LLM following the skill will use the delegation templates, not the appendix narrative.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\nNone identified. The fix is to orchestrator-facing delegation templates. No code imports or calls into this skill file.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\nNone identified.\\n\\n### Tests\\nNone identified. No test files cover add-new-feature Phase 1 or Phase 2 terminal output contract behavior. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Absence of test coverage means the fix cannot be regression-tested; a future edit could reintroduce the stale line 173 prose without a failing test to catch it.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/plan-artifact-lifecycle.md` \u2014 Line 63 references \\\"add-new-feature/SKILL.md Phase 1 and Phase 3 delegation prompts\\\" as sources for research artifact primary-source fetching protocol. This reference is about URL-following behavior, not terminal output contracts. The fix does not change what line 63 refers to. | Risk: LOW | Why: The reference will remain accurate after the fix. No update needed.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 Lines 297\u2013303 describe the add-new-feature 6 internal phases at a high level (\\\"Phase 1: feature research, Phase 2: codebase analysis\\\"). Does not describe terminal output contracts or artifact registration ownership. | Risk: LOW | Why: The high-level phase description is unaffected by terminal output contract changes. No update needed.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\nNone identified. No CI workflows test add-new-feature Phase 1 or Phase 2 behavior.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/feature-researcher.md` \u2014 Step 7 instructs \\\"Return DONE or BLOCKED status to orchestrator.\\\" The `<success_criteria>` Level 1 checklist includes `STATUS: DONE or BLOCKED returned to orchestrator`. The agent's own definition is already aligned with the fix direction. After the SKILL.md fix adds the explicit `STATUS: DONE` instruction to the Phase 1 delegation template, the orchestrator's prompt to the agent will now explicitly require this behavior, matching what the agent already does. | Risk: LOW | Why: No behavioral change in the agent file itself \u2014 the fix closes the gap in the orchestrator-facing delegation template. VERIFY_COMPATIBLE.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/codebase-analyzer.md` \u2014 Step 5 instructs \\\"Return a brief confirmation. DO NOT include document contents.\\\" The `<structured_returns>` block specifies `STATUS: DONE` with artifact IDs only. The agent definition is already aligned with the fix direction. After the SKILL.md fix updates the Phase 2 delegation template (removing stale line 173 prose), the orchestrator's contract matches what the agent already does. | Risk: LOW | Why: No behavioral change in the agent file itself \u2014 the fix removes the contradicting prose from the orchestrator skill. VERIFY_COMPATIBLE.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` | Role: producer | Connection: primary file being edited \u2014 contains both the stale Phase 2 line 173 prose and the missing Phase 1 STATUS: DONE instruction | Action: MULTIPLE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'returns the content in its response'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/feature-researcher.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: Phase 1 agent; its `<success_criteria>` already specifies STATUS: DONE return; fix aligns orchestrator template with agent contract | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/codebase-analyzer.md` | Role: consumer | Connection: Phase 2 agent; its structured returns already specify STATUS: DONE with artifact IDs only; fix removes contradicting orchestrator-side prose | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/plan-artifact-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: references SKILL.md Phase 1 delegation prompt for research artifact URL-following \u2014 unaffected by terminal output contract fix | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: describes add-new-feature phases at high level \u2014 no terminal output contract claims | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n- Overall system risk: LOW\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 both changes are in this single file; contradictory prose at line 173 and missing STATUS: DONE at Phase 1 end\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - behavioral (orchestrator reads inline phase content instead of only STATUS: DONE \u2014 the documented root cause)\\n  - documentation (stale line 173 contradicts the Phase 2 delegation template three lines below it)\\n  - verification (no test coverage guards against regression of the fix)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - None. Fix is contained to add-new-feature/SKILL.md. Both agent definitions (feature-researcher, codebase-analyzer) are already aligned with the fix direction. The two documentation files that reference Phase 1 reference unrelated behavior and need no changes.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n- [x] Every code producer updated or verified compatible \u2014 SKILL.md is the sole producer; agent files are already aligned\\n- [x] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible \u2014 feature-researcher and codebase-analyzer agent definitions already specify STATUS: DONE returns\\n- [x] Every affected test updated or new coverage added \u2014 no tests exist; absence noted as verification risk\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated \u2014 line 173 of SKILL.md is the stale content; this is the primary fix target\\n- [x] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated \u2014 none affected\\n- [x] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated \u2014 agent files require no changes\\n- [x] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved \u2014 not applicable (no replacement/migration)\\n- [x] Hidden transitive dependencies checked \u2014 no cross-plugin or cross-skill consumers found\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAlignment assessment: **DIVERGENT**\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n| Category | Proposed Change Excerpt | Mission Source Violated | Citation |\\n|----------|------------------------|-------------------------|----------|\\n| violates-design-principle | Phase 1 delegation template (lines 113\u2013147) ends with artifact registration metadata but lacks explicit \\\"return only STATUS: DONE\\\" instruction. Orchestrator has no contracted output guarantee and receives whatever content the agent returns. | \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff) \u2014 stored via artifact_register and retrieved via artifact_read, never via direct filesystem paths\\\" + \\\"MCP-native rule for agents: Agents store system artifacts exclusively via artifact_register with content=\\\" | CLAUDE.md lines 16, 147 |\\n| violates-design-principle | Phase 2 line 173 states: \\\"The agent returns the content in its response; the orchestrator then registers it as an artifact.\\\" This contradicts Phase 2 delegation template lines 188\u2013195 which instruct the agent to call artifact_register itself, and contradicts lines 204\u2013207 which state \\\"The orchestrator MUST NOT call artifact_register as a workaround \u2014 the MCP-native rule is that agents own their artifact storage.\\\" | \\\"MCP-native rule for agents: Agents store system artifacts exclusively via artifact_register with content=\\\" + agents own artifact storage, orchestrator does not register on their behalf | CLAUDE.md line 147; SKILL.md lines 188\u2013195, 204\u2013207 |\\n| reverses-committed-direction | Phase 1 lacks the self-registration + STATUS: DONE pattern that was established and fixed in Phase 3 (commit 9ef49696, issue #2429). The item description notes this gap was discovered in live session #613 where orchestrator context pollution caused fabricated facts to be injected into Phase 3. Phase 3 was fixed to prevent this; Phase 1 and Phase 2 still have the vulnerability. | Phase 3 already implements correct pattern (lines 365\u2013381): artifact_register(content=...) call followed by explicit STATUS: DONE return. Same pattern must apply to Phase 1 and Phase 2. | commit 9ef49696; SKILL.md lines 365\u2013381, 533 |\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n3 concerns identified: 2 violates-design-principle, 1 reverses-committed-direction. Phase 1 delegation template is missing explicit STATUS: DONE terminal instruction (lines 113\u2013147); Phase 2 line 173 contains stale prose contradicting the self-registration rule established in Phase 3 fix (commit 9ef49696). Both phases allow artifact content to flow to the orchestrator's context window instead of staying within agent/artifact storage boundary defined by CLAUDE.md. Phase 3 is correctly aligned with design intent; Phase 1 and Phase 2 violate it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **refactor: add-new-feature phase 3 dispatches via task-worker proxy instead of dedicated architect agent** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 add-new-feature #613 planning 2026-05-24\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-24\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:caa1eefe511b9eb0aa3f63c6a6478a3d -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-24)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n- Goal: Remove stale and broken output contract instructions from Phase 1 and Phase 2 delegation templates in add-new-feature/SKILL.md so orchestrators cannot read or inject artifact content between phases\\n- Status: APPROVED-FOR-PLANNING\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - Functional intent: fix two delegation templates to match Phase 3's already-correct output contract\\n  - Scope boundary: single file (plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md), two surgical edits\\n  - Existing system context: Phase 3 reference implementation verified at commit 9ef49696; Phase 1 and Phase 2 current text verified by swarm\\n  - Verification expectations: each phase's delegation template can be read and compared against Phase 3 pattern\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Acceptance criteria: derivable from Phase 3 reference implementation (lines 365\u2013381) and the orchestrator-coordination design principle in CLAUDE.md\\n- MISSING: none\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Refactor\\n- Behavioral boundary: correctness is observed when Phase 1 and Phase 2 agents self-register their artifacts and emit explicit STATUS: DONE terminal output \u2014 the orchestrator receives only a file path reference, never inline artifact content\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: acceptance criteria must prove that neither Phase 1 nor Phase 2 delegation templates contain instructions that cause the orchestrator to hold artifact content inline or register artifacts on the agent's behalf\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Open `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md`\\n2. Read the Phase 1 delegation template (lines ~109\u2013158) \u2014 observe that the template includes only metadata parameters (artifact_type, artifact_id, item_id, agent) with no `artifact_register(content=...)` call block and no explicit STATUS: DONE terminal instruction\\n3. Read the Phase 2 delegation template (lines ~162\u2013210) \u2014 observe line ~173 where the prose states \\\"The agent returns the content in its response; the orchestrator then registers it as an artifact\\\" (orchestrator-side registration instruction)\\n4. Compare both against the Phase 3 delegation template (lines ~365\u2013381) \u2014 Phase 3 instructs the agent to call `artifact_register(content=...)` itself and emit STATUS: DONE; Phases 1 and 2 do not match this contract\\n5. Observe that Phase 2 lines 188+ correctly state self-registration is the agent's responsibility \u2014 confirming line ~173 is stale prose that directly contradicts the correct instruction in the same file\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 The stale instructions are active prompt engineering bugs that directly caused fabricated facts during session #613 planning. Any orchestrator following the Phase 1 or Phase 2 templates as written will either silently skip artifact registration (Phase 1) or attempt to hold and re-inject artifact content inline (Phase 2 line ~173). Both failure modes corrupt the planning pipeline for any feature that uses `add-new-feature`. The fix is bounded to two surgical edits in one file, making the cost-to-fix extremely low relative to the ongoing risk of corrupted planning sessions.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: any planning session using `add-new-feature` that exercises Phase 1 or Phase 2 \u2014 the orchestrator either skips artifact registration silently (Phase 1) or is instructed to hold artifact content inline between phases (Phase 2), violating the MCP-native coordination design principle\\n- Bottleneck: Phase 2 line ~173 is a direct contradiction within the same file \u2014 an LLM following the skill will resolve the conflict arbitrarily, making planning output non-deterministic\\n- Fabrication risk: confirmed in session #613 \u2014 stale instructions caused the orchestrator to inject artifact content it should not have held, leading to fabricated facts in the planning output\\n- Systemic: this is a recurring-pattern class issue; Phase 3 was fixed in isolation (commit 9ef49696, issue #2429) without a cross-phase audit, leaving Phase 1 and Phase 2 with the same defect\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Planning sessions using `add-new-feature` produce deterministic outputs \u2014 no phase's delegation template instructs the orchestrator to hold or re-inject artifact content\\n- All three phases use the same output contract, eliminating the within-file contradiction that currently makes Phase 2 non-deterministic\\n- Removes the root cause of the session #613 fabrication event \u2014 future planning runs on the same skill path will not encounter the same failure mode\\n- Establishes a consistent cross-phase output contract pattern that can be referenced when future phases are added to the skill\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen the `add-new-feature` skill is followed by an orchestrator:\\n\\n- Phase 1 agent (`@dh:feature-researcher`) completes its work, calls `artifact_register(content=...)` itself to store the discovery artifact, and emits an explicit STATUS: DONE terminal message with a file path reference \u2014 the orchestrator receives only the path, never the artifact content inline\\n- Phase 2 agent (`@dh:codebase-analyzer`) follows the same pattern: self-registers its artifact via `artifact_register(content=...)` and emits STATUS: DONE with a file path reference \u2014 the orchestrator does not hold or re-register the content on the agent's behalf\\n- Both Phase 1 and Phase 2 delegation templates are consistent with Phase 3's already-correct output contract \u2014 an orchestrator following any of the three phases encounters the same instruction pattern\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Read `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` Phase 1 delegation template \u2014 the template contains an explicit STATUS: DONE terminal instruction directing the agent to emit STATUS: DONE with a file path reference after completing its work\\n2. Read Phase 1 delegation template \u2014 the template contains a full `artifact_register(content=...)` call block instructing the agent to self-register the artifact (not metadata-only parameters without a registration call)\\n3. Read Phase 2 delegation template \u2014 line ~173 (or equivalent prose) no longer contains any instruction directing the orchestrator to register the artifact on the agent's behalf; the phrase \\\"the orchestrator then registers it\\\" or equivalent is absent\\n4. Read Phase 2 delegation template \u2014 the template contains a full `artifact_register(content=...)` call block instructing the agent to self-register the artifact\\n5. Read Phase 2 delegation template \u2014 the template contains an explicit STATUS: DONE terminal instruction consistent with Phase 3's contract\\n6. Read Phase 3 delegation template (lines ~365\u2013381, reference implementation) and compare against Phase 1 and Phase 2 templates \u2014 all three phases contain structurally equivalent output contract instructions: self-registration call, file path reference, explicit STATUS: DONE\\n7. No internal contradiction exists within SKILL.md on the question of who registers Phase 1 or Phase 2 artifacts \u2014 every instruction in the file attributes registration responsibility to the agent, not the orchestrator\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | commit 9ef49696 \u2014 Phase 3 output contract fix (reference implementation for Phase 1 and Phase 2) |\\n| Backlog | issue #2429 \u2014 Phase 3 fix that was applied in isolation without cross-phase audit |\\n| Session observation | Session #613 (2026-05-24) \u2014 stale Phase 2 instruction caused orchestrator to inject artifact content inline, producing fabricated facts in planning output |\\n| Docs | CLAUDE.md MCP-native rule \u2014 orchestrators must never hold phase content inline; agents self-register via MCP artifact_register |\\n| Skill | `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` lines ~365\u2013381 \u2014 Phase 3 terminal output instruction (reference implementation pattern)\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md`\\n  - Phase 1 section: lines ~109\u2013158 (feature-researcher delegation template \u2014 missing artifact_register call and STATUS: DONE terminal instruction)\\n  - Phase 2 section: lines ~162\u2013210 (codebase-analyzer delegation template \u2014 stale line ~173 instructs orchestrator-side registration, contradicting lines 188+)\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 Phase 3 fix (issue #2429, commit 9ef49696) is already merged; the reference implementation is available\\n- Blocks: None identified \u2014 this is a self-contained correctness fix\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 two surgical edits to one SKILL.md file. Root cause is verified, scope is bounded, reference implementation exists (Phase 3 lines ~365\u2013381). No agent files, CI, configuration, or documentation changes required.\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n- [ ] feature-researcher.md agent body SOP (Steps 6/7) does not contain a self-registration step \u2014 agent dispatched outside add-new-feature pipeline will not call artifact_register(content=...) (reported by dh:multi-perspective-review on T0)\\n- [ ] Phase 2 STATUS block uses `path:` label for a logical artifact_id (codebase-{focus}-{slug}) not a filesystem path \u2014 Phase 1/3 use `path:` correctly; parenthetical clarification dropped during refactor (reported by dh:multi-perspective-review on T0)\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: recurring-pattern\\n**Rationale**: The same problem class \u2014 phase agent returning artifact content inline to the orchestrator instead of self-registering via `artifact_register(content=...)` \u2014 appears in at least three separate locations:\\n1. **Phase 2** (SKILL.md line 173): \\\"The agent returns the content in its response; the orchestrator then registers it as an artifact.\\\" \u2014 stale prose that contradicts the self-registration rule established for Phase 3.\\n2. **Phase 3** (#2429, commit 9ef49696): Same pattern. Fixed: architect agent now self-registers; orchestrator receives only `STATUS: DONE`.\\n3. **Phase 1**: No explicit `STATUS: DONE` terminal instruction \u2014 undefined output contract, same structural gap (orchestrator has no reliable signal that phase completed and artifact is durable).\\nLine 533 of SKILL.md documents the impact class explicitly: pre-#1527 pattern caused JSONL session output to grow to 300KB+, which the orchestrator cannot process. Session #613 produced fabricated facts and policy violations from the same cause.\\nQ2 fires before Q4: the same problem class appeared in 3 places, making this a systemic authoring gap, not a one-off oversight.\\n**Analysis Method**: 6-sigma RCA \u2014 enumerate all occurrences of the problem class across the skill file, trace to common root cause\\n**Scenario Target**: Phase agent returns content inline to orchestrator \u2192 orchestrator context floods, fabricated facts, policy violations -> All phase agents self-register artifacts and emit explicit `STATUS: DONE`; orchestrator output contract is documented at each phase\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n**Problem class**: Phase agents in `add-new-feature` lack a uniform output contract specifying self-registration and terminal `STATUS: DONE`.\\n\\n**Why 1**: Phase 2 prose at line 173 still says \\\"the orchestrator then registers\\\" \u2014 written before the self-registration pattern existed.\\n\\n**Why 2**: The self-registration fix was applied to Phase 3 in isolation (#2429) without auditing Phase 1 and Phase 2 for the same gap.\\n\\n**Why 3**: No shared output contract template or cross-phase quality gate exists to enforce consistent terminal instructions across all phases when one phase is updated.\\n\\n**Why 4**: `add-new-feature/SKILL.md` authoring treats each phase section as independent prose, not as instantiations of a shared agent-output-contract pattern.\\n\\n**Why 5**: The agent-output-contracts rule (`.claude/rules/agent-output-contracts.md`) defines the standard but is not enforced at skill-authoring time \u2014 no linting or review gate checks phase sections for `STATUS: DONE` terminal instructions.\\n\\n**Root cause**: Point fixes applied per phase without a cross-phase audit gate, combined with the absence of a shared output contract template that all phase delegation sections must satisfy.\\n\\n**Instances confirmed**:\\n- `add-new-feature/SKILL.md:173` \u2014 Phase 2 stale prose (orchestrator registers, not agent)\\n- `add-new-feature/SKILL.md` Phase 1 \u2014 missing `STATUS: DONE` terminal instruction\\n- Phase 3 \u2014 already fixed in commit 9ef49696 (#2429)\\n\\n**Fix scope**: Add explicit `STATUS: DONE` terminal contract to Phase 1 delegation; replace Phase 2 line 173 stale prose with self-registration instruction; verify Phase 4 and Phase 5 for same gap.\\n\",\"digest\":\"5332c20c0f2b5a92ef8c7ad5ae33f0bfb5baeb607b8e45facfdba71d493e7e47\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCP15A\"}"}